==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|grēv}}, {{IPA|en|/ɡɹiːv/}}
* {{homophones|en|grieve}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːv}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|greve}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|grǣfe}}, {{m|ang|græfe|grǣfa|bush, bramble, grove, thicket, copse, brush-wood (for burning), fuel}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|greve}}, {{m|sco|greave|t=grove}}. Compare also {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*grainiz||twig}}, of unknown origin, whence {{cog|non|grein||branch, bough}}. Closely related to {{cog|ang|grāf}}, {{m|ang|grāfa||grove}}. See {{l|en|grove}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[bush]]; a [[tree]]; a [[grove]].
#: {{rfquotek|en|Spenser}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[bough]]; a [[branch]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|greve}}, {{m|enm|greyve}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|grǣfa}}, {{m|ang|grēfa||pit, cave, hole, grave, trench}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*grōbō||pit, ditch}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*gʰrebʰ-||to dig, scratch, scrape}}. Cognate with {{cog|frr|groop||pit, sewer, gutter}}, {{cog|nl|groef||pit, hole, gutter}}, {{cog|de|Grube||pit, hole}}, {{cog|is|gröf||pit, grave}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[ditch]] or [[trench]].

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|greve}}, {{m|enm|grayve}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|greve||shin}}, of uncertain origin; possibly from {{etyl|arz|en}} {{m|arz|جورب||stocking, leg cover}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|greeve}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A piece of [[armour]] that protects the [[leg]], especially the [[shin]].

* {{seeCites}}

